---
// ── SEO COPY REWRITE APPLIED, 16 September 2026 ─────────────────────────
//
// This page has been rewritten against commercial-carpet-cleaning-copy-rewrite.md
// (workbook row 28, primary `commercial carpet cleaning services` 150/mo,
// cluster 470/mo). Section numbers in the comments below are that brief's.
// Implemented: approved title and meta, new H1 with the old one demoted to
// the hero subheading line, the mapped phrase in the opening paragraph, the
// approved CTA in all four places, the low-moisture sentence in stage five,
// a new office H2, the renamed premises and FAQ headings, the narrowed
// comparison table and the full-footprint town list.
//
// 🔴 ONE BLOCKING CHANGE REQUEST IS OPEN AND SHIPS UNRESOLVED. The approved
// CTA, "Book a night clean", contradicts this page's own pricing section and
// BFD §13.2. It is PLACED, per the brief's Rule 4, and it SHOULD NOT GO LIVE
// until the workbook owner rules on the wording. Suggested replacement:
// "Get a quote for a night clean." See `primaryCta` below.
//
// Not done here, because they are not copy: the URL mismatch (workbook wants
// /commercial-cleaning/commercial-carpet-cleaning, the build is flat — a
// redirect decision for the build team), the canonical domain, FAQ schema,
// the header's competing "Get an instant quote", and hiding the testimonial
// block until real quotes exist.
//
// Commercial carpet cleaning — a LEAF page under the /commercial-cleaning/
// hub (navigation.ts, "Commercial services" group). Sibling of
// /commercial-cleaning/commercial-pressure-cleaning/, built in the same pass and sharing its
// structure deliberately: two leaves off one hub that open differently read
// as two different sites.
//
// NO CONTENT BRIEF EXISTED WHEN THIS PAGE WAS BUILT. One does now, and it's
// named at the top of this file. The note below is the original build's.
//
// NO CONTENT BRIEF EXISTS. Every other page in src/pages/ was built from a
// file in content-plans/; there is none for this one. All copy below is
// DRAFTED, not supplied, and needs sign-off before launch. Where a claim
// depends on a fact only TLB holds, it ships as a visible [bracket] per
// house convention rather than as a plausible guess.
//
// Structure follows commercial-cleaning.astro (hero, trust bar + definition
// under one backdrop, pathway cards, "worth knowing", related services,
// comparison, testimonials, service area, two FAQ blocks, closing CTA). The
// one addition is the scope-of-work block, which the hub flags in its own
// source as its highest-value missing content. A single service is far
// easier to scope than a whole contract, so it lands here first.
//
// BLOCKS LAUNCH:
//   - IS THIS DELIVERED IN HOUSE? The biggest unknown on the page. Hot water
//     extraction needs a truck-mount or a commercial portable, and nothing in
//     the brand foundation document or any existing page says TLB owns one.
//     If this work is subcontracted to a carpet specialist, the comparison
//     table below (whose first row is "employed by TLB, not subcontracted")
//     contradicts the page it sits on and must be dropped from this page
//     rather than reworded. See the §10 comment.
//   - Method. The scope block names hot water extraction AND encapsulation.
//     Confirm TLB offers both, or cut the second card and the sentence in
//     non-branded FAQ 2 that offers it as an alternative.
//   - Drying time, pricing basis and minimum charge are all bracketed. They
//     are the three things a facilities buyer rings about.
//   - [insured] and [police-checked] in card 3 are inherited from the hub,
//     where the same brackets are already open. One answer fixes both pages.
//
// NOT BUILT, deliberately: before/after slider, hero video, credibility
// signals. Carpet is the best before/after subject on the whole site and
// BeforeAfter.astro is already running on five pages, but it takes real
// paired photography and none exists for commercial work. The photo brief
// is written out at the reserved slot in the render below.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/sections/SiteHeader.astro';
import Hero from '../../components/sections/Hero.astro';
import TrustBar from '../../components/sections/TrustBar.astro';
import Backdrop from '../../components/ui/Backdrop.astro';
import TextBlock from '../../components/sections/TextBlock.astro';
import PathwayCards from '../../components/sections/PathwayCards.astro';
import ServiceBlocks from '../../components/sections/ServiceBlocks.astro';
import TagCloud from '../../components/sections/TagCloud.astro';
import ComparisonTable from '../../components/sections/ComparisonTable.astro';
import GoogleReviewSlider from '../../components/sections/GoogleReviewSlider.astro';
import { googleBusinessProfileUrl, googleReviews } from '../../data/googleReviews';
import Faq from '../../components/sections/Faq.astro';
import CallToAction from '../../components/sections/CallToAction.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../../components/sections/SiteFooter.astro';
import logoImg from '../../assets/logo/tlb-logo-horizontal.png';
// The §1 hero and §3 card 1 are this page's own, generated per
// IMAGE-GUIDELINES; the prompts are in
// src/assets/commercial_cleaning/carpet/IMAGE-PROMPTS.md.
//
// Cards 2 and 3 are REUSED from the hub's folder, per IMAGE-GUIDELINES §5
// ("reuse before generating; same subject = same file"). Neither card is
// about carpet: card 2 is the walkthrough where a scope gets agreed and card
// 3 is who is in the building, which is the same subject the hub uses those
// two frames for. Card 1 was briefly the hub's vacuuming photo and is now a
// carpet-specific frame, because it is the one card whose subject IS carpet
// and reusing there would have put the same photo on two pages meaning two
// different things.
// §2 backdrop, behind the Cream scrim — the homepage's, same as the hub.
import footerLogoImg from '../../assets/logo/tlb-logo-square.png';
import {
  primaryNav,
  headerNav,
  headerSecondaryCta,
  footerServiceLinks,
  footerContact,
  footerCopyright,
  quoteCta,
} from '../../data/navigation';
import { comparisonColumns, serviceComparisonRows } from '../../data/comparison';
import { footprintLocationGroups, footprintSubheading } from '../../data/locations';
import { trustBarCards } from '../../data/trust';
import { howItWorks, managedForYou, theLineBody } from '../../data/brand-lines';
import { walkthroughCtaLabel } from '../../data/premises';
import { localBusiness } from '../../data/business';
import { z } from 'astro/zod';
import { getPage } from '../../lib/cms';
import * as section from '../../lib/cms-schemas';
import { sized } from '../../lib/cms-schemas';

// §0 Metadata. Focus keyword leads the title, matching the hub's pattern.
//
// In `pageMeta`, on "Commercial Carpet Cleaning Services | TLB Cleaning":
// Approved title and meta, pasted verbatim from the brief (workbook row 28)
// per its Rule 3. The one added word, "Services", is what turns the head
// term into the mapped primary `commercial carpet cleaning services`
// (150/mo, Keyword Mapping & Metadata 15 Sep 2026).
// In `pageMeta`, on `description`:
// 140 characters. The live one was 173 and would have been truncated.
//
// ⚠️ CHANGE REQUEST, brief §3: this carries "commercial carpet cleaning"
// but not the full mapped phrase, so the meta component cannot score full
// marks. One added word fixes it and keeps the length at 149. Pasted as
// approved rather than edited, per Rule 3.
// In `pageMeta`, on `canonical`:
// ⚠️ Placeholder domain, same as every sibling page. Confirm before launch.

// §7 The primary CTA, defined once and repeated in four places per BFD §9.5:
// the hero button, pathway card 1, the button after the six stages and the
// closing block. The page already standardised to a single label, so this is
// a substitution rather than a cleanup.
//
// 🔴 BLOCKING — DO NOT PUBLISH WITHOUT A RULING. See the file header. The
// label below is the workbook's approved CTA, placed exactly as specified per
// the brief's Rule 4, and it contradicts the page it sits on: §22 says
// commercial work is priced on the site, the closing heading says "Start with
// a walkthrough", and BFD §13.2 says instant booking works badly where
// condition drives the price. Suggested replacement for the workbook:
// "Get a quote for a night clean."

// In `business`, on `areaServed`:
// ⚠️ NSW only, following the hub's call and this page's meta description.
// footerContact still claims the Southern Gold Coast. Same open item.

// §1 Hero. The kicker carries the focus keyword so someone arriving from a
// search result gets confirmation above the H1, which is free to be a
// benefit rather than a keyword. Same call the hub makes.
//
// CTA is "Book a site walkthrough", not the sitewide "Get an instant quote".
// Carpet is priced on area, fibre and condition, none of which survive a
// form, and branded FAQ 5 below says exactly that.
//
// A SCENE PHOTO, not a cut-out figure, following the call the hub made at
// TLB's direction: a studio cut-out on a plain background says "a cleaner"
// without saying commercial. Here the extractor, the desk bank and the
// glass-partitioned meeting room are what carry it.
//
// So this is family A in IMAGE-GUIDELINES terms, which sets two things:
//   - `imageFit` is UNSET on the render below, taking Placeholder's `cover`
//     default. There is no transparency to preserve and no figure to stand
//     on the section edge.
//   - `ratio` is 1/1 because the SOURCE is 1024x1024, so a box matching the
//     file crops nothing. Regenerate square if this is ever reshot, or
//     change both together.
//
// ⚠️ GENERATED, NOT PHOTOGRAPHED. An AI image of a person who does not
// exist, in the §3 canonical uniform (aqua polo, dark teal apron, teal
// gloves). Honest as generic service imagery and NOT presented as a named
// team member, which IMAGE-GUIDELINES §7 forbids. A real photo of a real TLB
// cleaner on a real site would still do more, so treat this as a good
// placeholder rather than the finished job.
//
// In `hero`, on `headingLines`:
// Brief §4. The exact phrase `commercial carpet cleaning services` is worth
// 15 points scored present or absent, and the old line is good writing that
// still lands one line lower.
// In `hero`, on `subheading`:
// Brief §5. Rule 5's second slot, which this page did not have. An earlier
// note here said Hero had no subheading prop; it does — Hero.astro renders
// `subheading` directly beneath the <h1> and above the lead, outside the
// heading, which is exactly where this belongs.
//
// Its extension is the PREMISES TYPE, distinct from the H1's timing, and it
// puts the four buyer types above the fold. That supports
// `office carpet cleaning services` (200/mo) — the largest term on this row,
// larger than the mapped primary itself — and carries
// `carpet cleaning services for offices` (20/mo).
// In `hero`, on `lead`:
// Brief §6. The old paragraph repeated the H1's exact phrase and then the
// new subheading's premises list, so it said the same thing a third time
// directly beneath two lines that had already said it. What replaces it is
// the method choice, which the headings do not carry and which the process
// section spends a whole sub-section on. "Your carpet is the first thing
// anyone walks on" stays — it is the best opening line on the page.

// §2 Definition. The answer-target block for the focus keyword and the first
// place the phrase appears in body copy. Paragraph two does the synonym work
// ("carpet steam cleaning", "hot water extraction") so this one page can
// answer all three queries instead of three thin pages competing.
//
// In `definitionBody`, on paragraph 1:
// Brief §10. The opening paragraph is worth 10 of the 100 primary-keyword
// points and needs the mapped phrase inside the first two sentences. The
// phrase was here without the word "services", so two words fix it, and the
// third sentence keeps `commercial carpet` as a variant.

// §3 Pathway cards. The three questions a commercial buyer actually arrives
// with, in their own words. Same framing as the hub, re-pointed at carpet.
//
// Destinations are the hub, the quote flow and /why-tlb/. A leaf that links
// only to a quote form strands the reader who is not ready to ask for one.
//
// In `pathwayCards`, on "A cleaner drawing a carpet extraction wand along the worn traffic lane of an office corridor, a cleaned strip visible behind the wand":
// The cleaned strip behind the wand is deliberate and it is the
// reason this frame exists. It is ONE frame showing work in
// progress, not a before/after pair implying a specific job, which
// IMAGE-GUIDELINES §7 rules out.
// In `pathwayCards`, on "I want to know who is in the building" (description):
// ⚠️ Two brackets, both inherited from the hub. Same answer fixes both.

// §4 Scope of work. THE MOST IMPORTANT BLOCK ON THIS PAGE.
//
// commercial-cleaning.astro flags in its own source that the site tells
// readers four times to demand a written scope and never shows one. That gap
// is far easier to close for one service than for a whole contract, so it
// closes here first. "Worth knowing" below repeats the demand; this section
// is what makes the demand honest.
//
// ⚠️ EVERY ITEM IS DRAFTED against standard commercial practice, not supplied
// by TLB. Confirm each one and cut anything TLB does not actually do. An
// overstated scope is worse than a short one, because this is the block a
// client will hold us to.
//
// icon-grid over TextBlock's { list } shape: six distinct stages with a
// sentence each, not a flat task list. The list shape is right for "the
// twenty things we wipe"; this is a method.
//
// In `scopeItems`, on "Encapsulation when drying time is tight" (description):
// ⚠️ Confirm TLB offers this second method. If not, cut this card and
// the matching sentence in non-branded FAQ 2.
// Brief §14. Five added words ("It is a low moisture method") are what
// honours the approved meta's promise of low-moisture options that dry
// fast. This is the one passage on the page that delivers on it.
// In `scopeItems`, on "Groomed, dried and put back" (description):
// ⚠️ Real bracket. Drying time depends on the machine, the pile and the
// weather, and nobody has said what TLB's air movers can do.

// §5 "Worth knowing". The hub's equivalent block is the strongest copy on the
// site and it works by making the reader distrust the cheapest quote rather
// than by making claims about TLB. Same job here, different lever: on carpet
// the lever is not the Award, it is that the cheap method looks identical on
// the day and costs you the carpet.
//
// ⚠️ The warranty point is real and checkable. Major carpet makers do require
// periodic hot water extraction by a professional to keep a warranty intact,
// and many name a frequency. No interval is stated here ON PURPOSE, because
// it varies by brand and an undated specific goes stale silently. Worth a
// verification read by someone who prices carpet work.

// §17 NEW SECTION. Offices as a distinct case.
//
// Targets `office carpet cleaning services` 200/mo and `carpet cleaning
// services for offices` 20/mo — 220/mo combined, which is MORE monthly volume
// than the mapped primary carries on its own, and the page currently captures
// none of it. It names offices six times and never addresses one.
//
// Why this is an H2 and not the page's primary: `office carpet cleaning
// services` outranks the mapped primary on volume and was tested as a
// replacement. It loses because it is narrower than the page, which also
// serves clinics, venues and strata, and because the whole 470/mo cluster
// sits under the commercial umbrella. The mapped primary holds.
//
// Placed after "What a low carpet quote usually leaves out" so the buying
// argument comes first and the audience detail follows.

// §6 Related pages. NOT the hub's full fifteen-pill premises list, which
// would compete with the hub for the same links. These are the settings
// where commercial carpet is the actual problem, plus the two routes back up
// the cluster (the hub and the pressure cleaning sibling).
//
// ✅ Every href below is confirmed and nests under /commercial-cleaning/.
// The client's URL sheet of 17 Sep 2026 gives the slugs for every Commercial
// row it lists; factories and breweries were settled separately the same day,
// to the sheet's own convention. See premises.ts.

// §10 Comparison table. The shared rows from src/data/comparison.ts, now on a
// fourth page.
//
// ⚠️ CONDITIONAL, and this is the file header's first launch blocker. Row one
// is "Nine full-time cleaners employed by TLB, not subcontracted". If
// commercial carpet work is subcontracted to a carpet specialist, this table
// contradicts the page it is sitting on and has to come off this page. That
// is a question for TLB, not a rewrite.
//
// In `comparisonTable`, on `rows`:
// Brief §19, scorecard finding 7. "Who cleans your home" becomes "your
// premises" on a commercial page, and the holiday-lets row comes off: an
// office manager or a strata committee reading it here is being shown a
// table written for somebody else. Every other row is neutral and factual
// and is unchanged. See data/comparison.ts.

// §11 Branded FAQs. Written in the CUSTOMER'S voice, where "we" means the
// reader's business rather than TLB. That register is the hub's, and it is
// held here deliberately: a leaf that switches voice reads as bolted on.

// §12 Non-branded FAQs. The long-tail block, aimed at what people search
// rather than what they ask us.
//
// ⚠️ VERIFICATION PASS REQUIRED. These claims are checkable:
//   - Hot water extraction is the method most carpet manufacturers specify in
//     their maintenance requirements. Correct in substance. The interval
//     varies by brand, which is why none is stated.
//   - Bonnet cleaning can void some carpet warranties. Correct for several
//     major makers. Hedged to "some" and "can", deliberately.
//   - Cleaning Services Award 2020 (MA000022) penalty rates for weekends and
//     late shifts. Correct, and consistent with the hub, which says the same.
//   - Drying time is NOT given as a number anywhere in this block, because
//     the honest answer depends on equipment TLB has not specified.
// Recommended before launch: a read by someone who prices carpet work, and a
// visible "last reviewed" date, since the Award changes every 1 July.
//
// In `nonBrandedFaqItems`, on "How much does commercial carpet cleaning cost?" (answer):
// ⚠️ BRACKETED ON PURPOSE. The most searched question on the topic, and
// the page cannot answer it without a number from TLB. A "from $X per
// square metre" that turns out to be wrong is worse than no number, and
// the ACCC treats from-pricing as a claim.

// §13 Closing CTA. Same offer as the hero, worded as what actually happens.
//
// In `closingCta`, on `cta`:
// 🔴 This is the clearest place the conflict shows. The heading says start
// with a walkthrough and the button underneath says book a night clean.

// CONTENT lives in src/content/pages/commercial-cleaning/commercial-carpet-cleaning.yaml
// and is edited in Pages CMS ("Commercial carpet cleaning"). This file owns the layout. The
// notes above explain why each section says what it says; they stay here
// because Pages CMS rewrites the YAML on save and would drop them.
const page = await getPage(
  'commercial-cleaning/commercial-carpet-cleaning',
  z.object({
    seo: section.seo,
    // The label of `primaryCta` (§7 above). It links to the quote page in Site
    // settings. Empty falls back to the site-wide walkthrough label.
    ctaLabel: section.optional(section.text),
    hero: section.hero,
    // Its background photo sits behind the trust bar and this block together
    // (the Backdrop below), so it cannot be left empty.
    definition: section.textBlock.extend({ backgroundImage: section.backgroundPhoto }),
    // A card with no button of its own gets the page's main button.
    pathways: section.pathwayCards.extend({
      cards: z.array(section.pathwayCards.shape.cards.element.extend({ cta: section.optional(section.link) })).min(1),
    }),
    scope: section.iconGrid,
    worthKnowing: section.textBlock,
    offices: section.textBlock,
    // This page's own short list of settings, not the shared premises list.
    settings: section.tagCloud.extend({ note: section.optional(z.array(section.text)) }),
    // The columns and rows are the shared table in Site settings. The page
    // owns its framing and the label of the "who cleans" row.
    comparison: section.comparisonTable
      .pick({ heading: true, lead: true, cornerLabel: true })
      .extend({ whoCleansLabel: section.text }),
    reviews: section.sectionHeading,
    // The subheading falls back to the shared footprint line, and the towns
    // come from Site settings.
    whereWeClean: section.tagCloud.omit({ tags: true }).extend({ subheading: section.optional(section.text) }),
    // Heading only. The paragraphs are the shared brand lines.
    brandLine: section.sectionHeading,
    brandedFaq: section.faq,
    faq: section.faq,
    closingCta: section.callToAction,
  }),
);

const pageMeta = {
  ...page.seo,
  canonical: 'https://tlbcleaning.com.au/commercial-cleaning/commercial-carpet-cleaning/',
};

const primaryCta = { label: page.ctaLabel ?? walkthroughCtaLabel, href: quoteCta.href };

const business = localBusiness({
  description: pageMeta.description,
  // ⚠️ NSW only. See the note "In `business`" above.
  areaServed: 'Northern Rivers and Tweed, NSW',
  sameAs: googleBusinessProfileUrl ? [googleBusinessProfileUrl] : [],
});

const heroImage = page.hero.image && sized(page.hero.image, '1/1');

const pathwayCards = page.pathways.cards.map((card, i) => ({
  number: i + 1,
  ...card,
  image: sized(card.image, '4/3'),
  cta: card.cta ?? primaryCta,
}));
---

<Base title={pageMeta.title} description={pageMeta.description} canonical={pageMeta.canonical} business={business}>
  <SiteHeader
    logo={{ label: 'TLB Cleaning', src: logoImg }}
    primaryNav={primaryNav}
    serviceNav={headerNav}
    secondaryCta={headerSecondaryCta}
    cta={quoteCta}
  />

  <main id="main">
    <Hero
      variant="split-single-image"
      imagePosition="right"
      kicker={page.hero.kicker}
      headingLines={page.hero.headingLines}
      subheading={page.hero.subheading}
      lead={page.hero.lead}
      cta={page.hero.cta ?? primaryCta}
      image={heroImage}
    />

    {/* Trust bar and definition share ONE photo behind a Cream scrim, laid across
        both sections. 90%, raised from 75% on 29 Sep 2026. Measured over this
        frame's own darkest region under the copy column, 75% gave 4.22:1 — an
        AA FAIL. 90% gives 5.96:1. Backdrop.astro's `opacity` doc carries the
        method. Same treatment as the hub. */}
    <Backdrop image={page.definition.backgroundImage} tint="muted" opacity={90}>
      <TrustBar variant="card" cards={trustBarCards} />
      <TextBlock
        theme="muted"
        align="left"
        heading={page.definition.heading}
        body={page.definition.body}
        cta={page.definition.cta}
      />
    </Backdrop>

    <PathwayCards heading={page.pathways.heading} headingAccent={page.pathways.headingAccent} cards={pathwayCards} />

    <ServiceBlocks
      variant="icon-grid"
      heading={page.scope.heading}
      lead={page.scope.lead}
      items={page.scope.items}
      cta={page.scope.cta ?? primaryCta}
    />

    <TextBlock
      theme="dark"
      align="left"
      heading={page.worthKnowing.heading}
      body={page.worthKnowing.body}
      cta={page.worthKnowing.cta}
      backgroundImage={page.worthKnowing.backgroundImage}
    />

    {/* §17. New H2, per the brief. `light` between the dark "low quote"
        band above and TagCloud's brand fill below, so the page keeps
        alternating rather than stacking three filled bands. */}
    <TextBlock
      theme="light"
      align="left"
      heading={page.offices.heading}
      body={page.offices.body}
      cta={page.offices.cta}
      backgroundImage={page.offices.backgroundImage}
    />

    {/* ⚠️ RESERVED SLOT: before and after slider (BeforeAfter.astro).

        Carpet is the best before/after subject on this site and the component
        is already running on five pages, so this section would do more here
        than any paragraph above it.

        NOT BUILT because BeforeAfter has no reserved-slot mode by design: its
        `src` is required, since a comparison slider with nothing to compare
        has no meaningful empty state. It needs real paired photography and
        none exists for commercial work.

        THE PHOTO BRIEF: one traffic lane or corridor, shot from a fixed
        tripod position, before and after, with NOTHING moved between the two
        frames. A shifted camera or a chair pushed in reads as a glitch rather
        than as cleaning. Two or three pairs is plenty. Drop it in here,
        between "Worth knowing" and the related pages. */}

    <TagCloud
      heading={page.settings.heading}
      subheading={page.settings.subheading}
      tags={page.settings.tags}
      note={page.settings.note ?? []}
    />

    <ComparisonTable
      heading={page.comparison.heading}
      cornerLabel={page.comparison.cornerLabel}
      lead={page.comparison.lead}
      columns={comparisonColumns}
      rows={serviceComparisonRows(page.comparison.whoCleansLabel)}
    />

    <GoogleReviewSlider heading={page.reviews.heading} reviews={googleReviews} profileUrl={googleBusinessProfileUrl} />

    <TagCloud
      heading={page.whereWeClean.heading}
      subheading={page.whereWeClean.subheading ?? footprintSubheading}
      groups={footprintLocationGroups}
      note={page.whereWeClean.note}
    />

    {/* §1.3 The Line as a section heading, with §1.4 as its standfirst.
        The copy, and the reason this page gets `fast` rather than the other
        variant, both live in data/brand-lines.ts. Change it there, not here.

        `muted`, not `dark`: TagCloud above paints --color-brand and Faq below
        paints --color-surface, so Cream between them keeps the page
        alternating instead of stacking two dark bands.

        No CTA prop. §9.5 wants one primary CTA per page, repeated, and the
        closing CallToAction is directly under the FAQs. */}
    <TextBlock
      theme="muted"
      align="left"
      heading={page.brandLine.heading}
      body={[managedForYou.recurring, ...theLineBody.commercial, ...howItWorks.general]}
    />

    {/* Brief §23 and §24. The page carried two FAQ headings and the generic
        one made the split look like a build error. This block is about
        working with TLB, the next is about the service, and naming them that
        way makes both useful. Title Case also breaks BFD §9.2. */}
    <Faq heading={page.brandedFaq.heading} lead={page.brandedFaq.lead} items={page.brandedFaq.items} />
    <Faq heading={page.faq.heading} lead={page.faq.lead} items={page.faq.items} />

    <CallToAction
      variant="secondary"
      kicker={page.closingCta.kicker}
      heading={page.closingCta.heading}
      lead={page.closingCta.lead}
      cta={page.closingCta.cta ?? primaryCta}
    />
  </main>

  <SiteFooter
    logo={{ label: 'TLB Cleaning', src: footerLogoImg }}
    columns={[
      { title: 'Services', links: footerServiceLinks },
      { title: 'Locations', links: [{ label: 'View all locations', href: '/locations/' }] },
    ]}
    contact={footerContact}
    copyright={footerCopyright}
  />
</Base>
